extends /views/templates/layouts/_base

append variables
	- pagetitle = "Accessibility"
	- pagedescription = "Accessibility in Tablechart: every chart is also a real table, for screen readers, print and PDF, and a long table can have a summary."

block content
	.lane
		.container
			.page-header
				h1 Accessibility
				p.lead Tablecharts intends to be as inclusive as possible. Here are some accessibility benefits.

	+section("What a screen reader gets")
		p The table stays on the page. It is hidden from sight, but screen readers read it like any other table: the names of the rows and columns, and every value. The chart is hidden from screen readers, with #[code aria-hidden="true"], so they do not read everything twice.
		p This button shows the table that a screen reader reads out loud:

		.lang-toggle(role="group" aria-label="The table of the example")
			button.show-table(type="button" aria-pressed="false") Show the table
		+chart(null, '1')
			figure#a11y-table(data-chart="column" data-chart-table="print")
				figcaption
					b Apples picked
					br
					span thousands
				table
					tbody
						each row in [['Spring', '58'], ['Summer', '72'], ['Autumn', '70'], ['Winter', '41']]
							tr
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]

		script.
			document.querySelector('.show-table').addEventListener('click', function () {
				var figure = document.getElementById('a11y-table');
				var shown = this.getAttribute('aria-pressed') !== 'true';
				figure.setAttribute('data-table', shown ? 'always' : 'print');
				this.setAttribute('aria-pressed', String(shown));
			});

		p Give the chart a title with a #[code figcaption]. Tablechart makes it the name of the figure and of the table, with #[code aria-labelledby]. So a screen reader that jumps to the table also reads the title and the unit. If the figure or the table already has a name, with #[code aria-label], #[code aria-labelledby] or a #[code caption], it keeps that name.
		p A screen reader does not read a line break. So Tablechart puts a full stop before each #[code br] in the title, hidden from sight, and a screen reader pauses between the title and the subtitle. If the line already ends with a full stop, a question mark, a colon or a comma, nothing is added.
		p If the chart has no #[code figcaption], add #[code aria-label] to the figure, such as #[code aria-label="Apples picked, in thousands"].

	+section("A summary for a long table")
		p A table is a good alternative to a chart, up to a point. Someone who sees a chart of 60 months sees the highest month and the trend at a glance. Someone who listens to its table hears 60 names and 60 numbers, one after the other, and cannot remember a number from four rows back. To find the highest month, they have to go through every row.
		p A summary gives a listener what the chart gives at a glance: a few sentences with, for example, the highest and the lowest value, the change from the first value to the last, the trend, and the values far from it. You write the sentences, and Tablechart fills in the numbers from the table, formatted as on the chart.

		+codeblock('HTML').
			<div class="chart-summary">
				<p>The highest price was {highest.value}, in {highest.name}.</p>
				<p data-summary="trend">Without the outliers, the trend is {trend}: from {trend.from} to {trend.to}.</p>
			</div>

		p A screen reader reads the summary right after the title of the chart. The table stays, for a listener who wants every number. The summary is real text in the page, so it can be shown to everyone too, and translated like any other text.
		p On paper, the summary is often the only alternative a long table has: text that is hidden on the page does not reach a PDF, and a table of 60 rows can take a page of its own. See #[a(href='#in-print-and-pdf') In print and PDF].
		p Write a summary for every chart with more than about 15 values. See #[a(href=url('/features/summary.html')) Summary].

	+section("Numbers that do not fit")
		p Some charts do not show all their numbers: a stack with small parts, a narrow chart, a chart with many rows, or a line chart with lines close together. The numbers are still in the table, so screen readers read them. Pointing at a row, or tapping it, shows them in a tooltip.
		p To show the table under the chart as well, add #[code data-chart-table="always"] to the figure. This chart has three lines close together, so it leaves numbers out. The table under it has them all.

		+chart(null, '1')
			figure(data-chart="line" data-chart-table="always")
				figcaption: b Apples per tree
				table
					thead
						tr
							th Year
							th North
							th South
							th East
					tbody
						each row in [['2018', '41', '38', '35'], ['2019', '45', '44', '42'], ['2020', '48', '47', '49'], ['2021', '52', '50', '51'], ['2022', '55', '56', '53'], ['2023', '58', '57', '59'], ['2024', '61', '60', '62'], ['2025', '64', '66', '63']]
							tr
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]
								td= row[2]
								td= row[3]

	+section("In print and PDF")
		p On screen, the tooltip shows numbers that are left out. On paper there is no tooltip, so a chart that leaves numbers out also needs its table. #[code data-chart-table], or the #[code table] option for all charts, says when the table is shown under the chart:

		table
			thead
				tr
					th Value
					th On screen
					th In print and PDF
			tbody
				tr
					td: code never
					td Hidden
					td Hidden. The default.
				tr
					td: code auto
					td Hidden
					td Shown, if the chart does not show all its numbers, and the table has no more than 15 values
				tr
					td: code print
					td Hidden
					td Always shown
				tr
					td: code always
					td Shown
					td Shown

		p If your pages become PDFs, use #[code auto]. Then a table only takes room on the page when the chart needs it.
		p With #[code auto], a table with more than 15 values is not printed, and the summary is printed instead. A chart that has neither on paper has no alternative in the PDF. See #[a(href='#a-summary-for-a-long-table') A summary for a long table].

		+codeblock('JS').
			init({ table: "auto" });

		p Whether numbers are left out depends on the width of the chart. #[code auto] uses the width the chart has on the page.

	+section("Not only colour")
		p Lines are told apart by their points as well: filled and open circles, squares and diamonds. A line can also be dashed or dotted. The button shows the chart without colour, as some people see it.

		.lang-toggle(role="group" aria-label="Colour of the example")
			button.greyscale(type="button" aria-pressed="false") Without colour

		+style.
			.a11y-colours {
				--tablechart-color-1: #c2410c;
				--tablechart-color-2: #15803d;
				--tablechart-color-3: #1d4ed8;
				--tablechart-color-4: #a21caf;
			}
			.is-grey .demo {
				filter: grayscale(1);
			}

		.a11y-grey
			+chart(null, '5')
				figure.a11y-colours(data-chart="line" data-chart-legend)
					table
						thead
							tr
								th Year
								th North
								th(data-line="dashed") South
								th East
								th(data-marker="diamond open") West
						tbody
							each row in [['2016', '41', '30', '22', '12'], ['2019', '58', '39', '35', '20'], ['2022', '77', '52', '41', '33'], ['2025', '105', '61', '70', '45']]
								tr
									th= row[0]
									td= row[1]
									td= row[2]
									td= row[3]
									td= row[4]

		script.
			document.querySelector('.greyscale').addEventListener('click', function () {
				var grey = this.getAttribute('aria-pressed') !== 'true';
				document.querySelector('.a11y-grey').classList.toggle('is-grey', grey);
				this.setAttribute('aria-pressed', String(grey));
			});

		ul
			li A number inside a bar is dark or light, whichever reads on the colour of the bar.
			li If you set your own colours, check that they contrast enough with the background: 3:1 for bars and lines, and 4.5:1 for a number inside a bar.

	+section("The total of a donut")
		p If a donut has no total row, the number in the middle is the sum of the slices. Tablechart adds it to the table, in a hidden row at the bottom, so screen readers read it with a name: "Total, 74.5". The #[code totallabel] option sets the name.

	+section("Annotations")
		p The text of an annotation, such as #[code +24%] on a bracket, stays in the page under the table, hidden from sight. Screen readers read it there. The badge in the drawing is hidden from them.

	+section("Without JavaScript")
		p If JavaScript does not run, there is no chart. The table is then shown instead, with its title.
		p A chart with #[code data-chart-src] has no table until JavaScript has read the JSON file. If JavaScript does not run, or the file cannot be read, there is no table and no chart. Write the table in the HTML instead of as a JSON data source if you want to be sure that the data (as a table then) is always available.

	+section("Keyboard")
		p The table is the way to the numbers for screen readers, and they move through it with their own keys. The tooltip only works with a pointer or a tap. If people who use a keyboard, without a screen reader, must be able to read every number, show the table with #[code data-chart-table="always"].

	+section("Zoom and text size")
		p All text is HTML, sized in #[code em]. It follows the text size and the zoom of the browser, and stays sharp. Long names beside the bars of a bar chart wrap onto more lines.

	+section("Motion")
		p If the visitor has asked for reduced motion in their system settings, charts are shown without animation.

	+section("Language")
		p Set the #[code lang] attribute on the #[code html] element, such as #[code lang="en"]. Screen readers use it to read the text in the right language, and Tablechart uses it to format the numbers. See #[a(href=url('/features/numbers.html')) Numbers].

	+section("Standards")
		p Websites in the European Union must meet the European Accessibility Act, which refers to the standard EN 301 549, and through it to WCAG 2.1 at level AA. For a chart, the most important requirements are a text alternative (WCAG 1.1.1) and information that is available as structure (WCAG 1.3.1). A real data table meets both, and Tablechart keeps that table in the page. For a long table, a summary is the alternative a listener can use, and in a PDF it is often the only one.
		p Tablechart helps with these requirements, but whether a website meets them depends on the whole website. Test your own pages, for example with a screen reader.
